Make the echo controller anew when recording starts again for a call

AudioState starts the audio device's recording with the first stream that sends sound
and stops it with the last (AddSendingStream, RemoveSendingStream), so it stops at the
end of every call and starts again with the next. The audio processing and its echo
controller (AEC3) stay from one call to the next, and AEC3 kept the last call's
alignment of what was played with what is recorded: its render buffer, where the read
point stood, and its delay estimate. Playout starting before recording piled played
blocks up in it, which AEC3 notices only when it checks for excess render blocks
(EchoCanceller3Config buffering.excess_render_detection_interval_blocks: 250 blocks,
once a second) and then resets its buffer and finds the delay again from the echo.
Meanwhile the other side's speech went back to them: in a call lab with a room's echo,
from the start of every call after the first, 1.2 to 2 s ("Excess render blocks
detected", then "Applying total delay of ..."), where the first call, whose AEC3 was
made when recording first started at the processing's rate, found the delay in 0.5 s.

AudioState now makes the echo controller anew once the device has started recording
again (AudioProcessing::RestartEchoController, the part of ApplyConfig that turning the
echo canceller on runs: AudioProcessingImpl::InitializeEchoController, under both
locks). A new AEC3 does not process the microphone before played sound reaches it, and
on its first recorded block after that resets its render buffer to the delay the audio
device reports with every block (set_stream_delay_ms; PulseAudio's playout and
recording latencies, about 27 to 53 ms there), less its headroom, then finds the
delay from the echo as at the first call. Nothing else of the processing is touched:
the gain control and its level, the noise suppressor, the custom capture processing
(the application's denoiser and its state), whether the output is used
(set_output_will_be_muted) and the runtime settings stay as they were. A full
AudioProcessing::Initialize() would have started them all over.

Only a start of recording does it: a stream added while recording runs (another
connection's voice, a shared screen's sound), a track replaced or muted with the
stream still sending, and the audio device starting again by itself (another device
chosen, a sound server back, patch 0006) leave the echo controller as it is. A
microphone turned off by having the voice channel only receive stops the recording
when nothing else sends, and turned on again it is a start like a call's (the same
stale alignment). The processing's rate does not change in a call (every sending
stream is Opus at 48 kHz), and its channels are kept by wrapper patch 0011, so the
echo controller is not made anew in a call that way either.

AEC3's excess render check is left at once a second: checked more often it would
reset the buffer (and align again from the reported delay) on bursts of played blocks
in a call, and a reset at the start leaves nothing piled up for it to find.

diff --git a/api/audio/audio_processing.h b/api/audio/audio_processing.h
index a4f3f52..c7fd326 100644
--- a/api/audio/audio_processing.h
+++ b/api/audio/audio_processing.h
@@ -522,6 +522,14 @@ class RTC_EXPORT AudioProcessing : public RefCountInterface {
   virtual size_t num_output_channels() const = 0;
   virtual size_t num_reverse_channels() const = 0;
 
+  // Fogata: makes the echo controller anew, as turning it on does, and leaves
+  // every other part of the processing as it is (gain control, noise
+  // suppression, the custom processing, whether the output is used, runtime
+  // settings). For the sound of a call starting after the audio device stopped
+  // recording: the echo controller kept from the last call holds that call's
+  // alignment of what was played with what is recorded. Takes both locks.
+  virtual void RestartEchoController() {}
+
   // Set to true when the output of AudioProcessing will be muted or in some
   // other way not used. Ideally, the captured audio would still be processed,
   // but some components may change behavior based on this information.
